/** simanim 501–697 stragglers — 84 blocks (64 overrides) */
export const TRANSLATIONS = {
  "501|rabbi-akiva-eiger|7|_": "Seif 7 in the gloss: even on the second day of Rosh Hashanah. It puzzles me why this is mentioned regarding Rosh Hashanah of Yom Tov. If we come to be stricter on Rosh Hashanah more than other Yom Tov, this is because Rosh Hashanah is one sanctity. And in any case it is permitted here as on the day itself he prepared. And an egg born that day is forbidden — see Magen Avraham siman 477 regarding second day of Rosh Hashanah or second day chol, or one sanctity — see there. And here in any case what is stringent about hides is only from one sanctity.",
  "502|rabbi-akiva-eiger|4|_": "Seif 4: in new pots. See above siman 503 seif 3 and Magen Avraham there.",
  "514|rabbi-akiva-eiger|5|_": "Seif 5: It is forbidden to light a candle that is lit for a dead person for seven days — see Mishbetzot Zahav above siman 532 at end of seif. To allow lighting by a non-Jew and the candle will be on Yom Tov in a room where they do not eat.",
  "515|chokhmat-shlomo|6|_": "(Seif 6) In a city where there is a large majority, obviously everyone who brings, for the sake of the majority, brings, etc. P.S. Refer to my essays on Yod, 17, 17, 17, 18, 19, 20, 30, 30, 30, 30, 30, 30, 30, 30, 30, 30, 30, 30, 30, 30, 30, 30, 30, 30, 30, 30, 30, 30, 30, 30, 30, 30.",
  "518|rabbi-akiva-eiger|2|_": "Seif 2 in the gloss: through a non-Jew — see above siman 532 seif 1 in the gloss and Magen Avraham there.",
  "527|rabbi-akiva-eiger|12|_": "Seif 12 and blesses the unleavened bread. He blesses the first blessing for both of them and says about the matzot of Irubin 3 from Haram Di Buton Seman 38 (also recorded above Semen 667):",
  "527|rabbi-akiva-eiger|22|_": "Seif 22 can involve a guarantor on the condition, etc. And if there is a wise man in the city who has given credit to all the citizens of the city. MM will condition and not rely on the wise man's involvement. And it is also seen from the words of Hacham Zvi in ​​the answer C. K. L.:",
  "531|rabbi-akiva-eiger|4|_": "Seif 4: And those who shave. And mourning for his father and mother when the time of grief arrived in Chol HaMoed — see Knesset HaGedolah, responsa Noda B'Yehudah, responsa Shevet Yom Tov, Chayei Adam siman 12.",
  "531|rabbi-akiva-eiger|8|_": "Seif 8: And there is no concern regarding shaving. And likewise all washing is permitted.",
  "533|rabbi-akiva-eiger|3|_": "Seif 3: It is permitted to buy. This is the first answer of the Rosh in Pesachim — that part of the land was deshaira and fish should taste damatakhli by itzla deshani maziya dalikha pasida. But merchandise is a loss — therefore forbidden to buy. But to buy them is merchandise and permitted. But the other answer of the Rosh is that only through buying and selling is it permitted, for there is no melacha and no tircha. But salting fish is melacha and tircha — therefore forbidden to buy and salt. Therefore the Tur should have ruled leniently. It appears the Rambam holds like the Rosh's first answer; see Magen Avraham note 16 and Rosh at end of Pesachim.",
  "537|rabbi-akiva-eiger|7|_": "Seif 7: From tree to tree. Rashi and Tur: in the field of the owner. And the Hagah Melech writes in the name of Ri'etz Giat: in the field of a field-worker. And in Beit Yosef he brought Ri'u's words: close like the way of planting from tree to tree four amot per se'ah — but not far. And likewise in Yerushalmi. And in Biur HaGra he brought Ri'u's words that close means like the way of planting — see there.",
  "538|rabbi-akiva-eiger|6|_": "Seif 6: We do not fine him after. It is possible if his father did not do the melacha — the law is he should not do so to \"levar bevaro\" — but if he already did, the law is dempeirotim — it is possible if he died, the son does not inherit and is not better than another, since it already became hefker. And for the Mechaber's intent if it is a lost item he need not fine if his father did the melacha — if so, even if his father did, the son is not liable after him.",
  "539|rabbi-akiva-eiger|11|_": "Seif 11 in the gloss: and it is permitted to buy. In Tur it is written: permitted to prepare means a duffle in the hands of dalai b's workshop such as cooking and the like is permitted.",
  "539|rabbi-akiva-eiger|5|_": "Seif 5: As mitzil from his hand. See Yoreh De'ah siman 148 seif 1 and Shach there.",
  "545|chokhmat-shlomo|3|_": "Seif 3. A person writes tefillin and mezuzot for himself, etc. NB Zal Rambam 17 from the 19th Yot 13 and it is forbidden to write the like. But a person writes tefillin and mezuzots for himself and weaves blue for his garment and wrote the 20m there it seems that his taste is DML Dain put tefillin in the humam per the rule of the Yot he and so it is not permissible to write tefillin more than another craft etc. Belongs to the Hoham, and I answered that it is not necessary for the time, because it is possible to give the house as a gift to someone else, and it will be a part of it in the Torah of Shala and exempt from the mezuzah. Likewise, the Magen Avraham said on behalf of his brother above in the Halachot Tzizit, Mark 13, and what the Datirz Hatem does not belong here, the Hoham, it is permissible to give as a gift, and yet it is not necessary for the time. With a clean tefillin, even though he can borrow from others from anywhere, because he is required to wear a tefillin, a person wants to go out on his own. To fulfill a mitzvah with his money is a necessity of the time. And from this the Dharambam Sal Dasor proves in the Homam, but in the Mezuzah, if he gives a gift after the house, it is not considered a need of the time, and more than that, the Magen Avraham wrote in the sign of the TAMV, and since he wants to burn it, he does not violate it As Man David and Ak Nihu Del Lat does not commit compel, as per the fulfillment of the mitzva, and as such, the prohibition to the prohibition of Shabbat, as per the fulfillment of the Mitzva, is not transgressed. He does not die without a deed, but in what is not necessary for him to get rid of it, he is raped like Man David and for that with leaven, then if he did not have leaven he is exempt and there is no obligation to have leaven and even if he has leaven he can burn it by someone else and it is not an obligation in his body precisely for this reason the compel makes as if they actually committed the act and he is as the fulfillment of the actual deed. And therefore with Shafir Dovmezuza there is an obligation for him to have a house in which to make a mezuzah and there is no mitzvah in his body And what is the mission in him for that the compel was done as if the act exists and therefore the time is not necessary to be reversed if it is forbidden to write it will be permissible for a generation in it without a mezuzah dachion danus is hoi as the actual performance of the act and thus he is exempt from it and also with a blue teva for his clothing but with a tefillin it is a faded obligation precisely and it is not possible to exempt in any way a mitzvah in his body rather than mehani by a messenger so what is daunsa like Man David akf In his body, I will not commit compel in this either, since there is a charge for it. If there is a charge, it is due to the time and the reason for the penalty. And proof of our words from the Masash in the Shas of Baruch, a man was forced to do a mitzvah and compel and he did not do it, the scripture says that they did such things and why on Passovers do they accidentally or compel and the first did not do it and did the second and it is obligatory to cut if the second did not do it, because it is compel, if such a person did it. In fact, there is a difference between a mitzva that is actually obligatory or one can get rid of it and one who thinks to do a mitzva from a miry in a mitzva. It is possible to be exempted by Hashem and you will know by Hashem.",
  "547|chokhmat-shlomo|3|_": "Seif 3. Every thirty days before the due date, it is forbidden to eulogize a dead person who died before thirty days before the due date, etc. NB Here is the source of this division between a person who died within a day or before the time of his death from the Jerusalemite brought by the 2nd Shem and the late Jerusalemite shall not wake up for his death, etc. We shall not eulogize first on the occasion of the the festival day, the one who performs eulogy for him in the Old Testament, but on the new one, something new is allowed within the the festival day, an old day, after the the festival day, the Jerusalemite Yom Akal. And here the B.I. realized that his intention was between the day of the day and the day of the day of Moed, whether he died within the day of the day of Moed or not. And from this, it was necessary for the taste of Dakhel to go to the Midd on a day in the Hashem, O the bitterness in his heart, etc. And for Panad, in my opinion, it does not seem like this, finally, if all of his Midd within the Moed, A.K.A.F. And yet he still cancels the mitzvah of joy from the last Mitzvah, and why wouldn't he feel the need to observe what is possible for this? And they could not do the Passover on that day, and yet they became impure, even though they knew they could not do the Passover, yet they became impure and did not fear the loss of doing the Passover, and the interpretation of the Passover there, they actually died from the mitzva. Tarvaihu dai from Pesach is the sd because it is not at the time required for Passover and is not the sd, it is the sd precisely in regard to the matter in which there is an obligation to wash, etc. The mitzvah, and in particular, the time of obligation of the Harnel joy, but the main point of the mitzvah is exemption from the mitzvah, we are only that it is impossible to fulfill both, but if it is possible to fulfill both, it is not exempted, and therefore in the obituary, one can eulogize the habit for that, it does not reject the joy of the habit. But according to Paz it is known that the main part of the obituary is at the time of heating and the main part of the obituary is within thirty days for mourning, but after thirty days there is such a warm-up and therefore exemption from obituary in the hearer after the third day, the hour of heating and therefore exemption even from tearing, and therefore the obituary which is done within thirty days will not be done so much after thirty days and therefore it is impossible to hold both and therefore it is permissible to perform eulogy and he does not have to feel the joy of the habit even within the habit itself Of course it is forbidden to eulogize him, since eulogy is d'rabbanan and the joy of the Hagit Dauriita is not a mitzvah d'rabbanan rejects a mitzvah made Dauriita. But we were just like this, when the time of Hashem's creation was established, then, O, the displacement of Hashem in the resurrection and deed, there is no power in their hands to uproot, even if it is considered as Shabbat and A't from any place, so the Ram was brought at the time of the day, they have power in their hand to uproot Hashem even in Sho'at, only the fact that it is not in the era, but in this era, there is no power in the hand of the Sages to uproot the Hashem even in the Shoah, and the A'k, because the Dakbar applies to it happy A leg does not have the power in their hand to dislodge Hashem, even if it is considered a Sho'at, but the foot is still not from time to time, a Dyot is imposed, and as it was said in the above-mentioned Shas Dasukah on Passover, in this there is power in the hand of the Sage to dislodge in the Sho'at, so now with the permission of his eulogy, and after that he does not observe the Yot's joy, be thin in the Sho'at, in this there is power in the hand of the Sage Shafir, and this is what the Jerusalemite said in you In the old, we used to say that the obituary was for an old dead person who is already thirty years after his death, but such an obituary can be done later, it is not allowed to postpone the Habit Hajim by him, but in the new one, if the obituary is for a new dead person within the year of his death, this should not be considered as canceling the Harenal Deusk's Mitzvah. The other is the meaning of the Jerusalem language for the Panad. After writing this, I saw in Hanaon Moharaz's answer that the Ramban already harnished this Jerusalem interpretation, but he was pushed to a portion of the power of the devout of the blaha. Yes, and all of this is pressing. But per the PMSh, it is fair to say that it is right in its taste, and there is no contradiction at all to the opinion of the rabbi, only that the one who engages in a mitzvah is exempt from the mitzvah. And for this reason, since dahlakta is a rabbi in my prohibitions, only this one was ruled as Moel, on behalf of the fact that they bring evidence from the Jerusalemite, and since Daldidan is a rabbi's evidence from a Jerusalemite, that is why they have ruled that the dahlakta is a rabbi in my prohibitions, and in any case, it is not forbidden only for a fee, but for free is allowed. And I relied on that and made a eulogy for a wise man in the year 166 of that year A. Before the Madd for the Pilgrimage and the eulogy was within the Madd for the Pilgrimage and together with all my opinions, as well as the ruling of the Gaon Moharaz ztzel and I held his words more and ZAV to the Panad and from the words of the Ramban himself there is evidence according to Dahri, he is a Sal Davt to the Madd the mourner is not renewed by the obituary and now a Dsal Davt to the Madd is considered as the actual hour of death and of course it is not similar to a warm-up of the hour of her death later, and therefore it is not considered possible to observe both what we will say after a day's medical examination and check carefully:",
  "551|chokhmat-shlomo|10|_": "Seif 10 of the HaHa and at a mitzvah meal such as a word and the redemption of the son and the conclusion of a tract, etc. P.S. Refer to the 16th Skib of the MS regarding the meal of a word, which seems to me to be a complete permission since a word rejects Shabbat and Shabbat rejects a building in the HCMC, etc. And his words, you will understand that in the Shas, in the MK, he will turn the name of the HC, a sham, from a house that rejects the Shabbat of the HC in the HC, and in the same way, why should we be afraid, and secondly, from the fact that I have a higher need than the needs of a layman, etc., so it is proven by the needs of a layman that I have a higher need, so it is expressed to reverse the words of the 16th, that in truth it is not for the KU, this is a law of the KV, even if the sin is later forgiven them, there is no evidence It is not a sin to say that there was a sin only by those who repented and did penance, they are forgiven, and as the Sages said, everyone who commits a transgression and is ashamed of it immediately is forgiven, and the words of the 16th Psalm and daf",
  "551|chokhmat-shlomo|3|_": "Seif 3. A week in which the Tabernacle falls is forbidden to tell, etc. NB 25 B.S. Taanit page L. Raba said Halachh Karshbg and A. Raba Halachh Karm and the interpretation of it to Kola A.S. From Shabbat Ach Kesha Darba DSL Dainu is forbidden except for the week that ends TAB until the Lent AK as Man Drish if Drish Shabbat is forbidden the whole Shabbat and if Dharsh Haga is forbidden Marah and Man'l it seems to be interpreted as Mahga and Pirshii Darach Ikri Moed A'Thih Marah the prohibition begins but Man'l Dakhla the prohibition in Tv Dalma every this month. And his intention seems to be that it is clear in the puskims and Lakman in the siman 19 Ikri Moed and so on, like the days of Passover and the days of the holiday are called all the days that meanwhile in the language of a holiday even faded only one day at the beginning and one day at the end. Yes, these are the 9 days from Ramah to Lent, since the beginning of them is Rabh Dakri, a day, and its end is called a day, and therefore all the days are called in the language of Hagg, and for this reason, Yalif Magha is forbidden, and it is Marah until Lent. Daino is not allowed on the Shabbat only that Shabbat and I have left studies for Kola. Yes, it seems to the Panad that it is correct and very accurate:",
  "551|rabbi-akiva-eiger|9|_": "Seif 9: Such as for a sick person. In responsa Shevet Yom Tov ch. 1 siman 107: to be lenient for a niddah who is slightly ill.",
  "552|rabbi-akiva-eiger|7|_": "Seif 7: On land. And Maharikash wrote: one must change a place where he is accustomed to eat. Tosafot Taanit 41a and Levush siman 599 seif 1. See there.",
  "554|chatam-sofer|18|_": "Seif 18 in the gloss: and some say marital relations — see responsa Ketav Sofer siman 101.",
  "554|rabbi-akiva-eiger|22|_": "Seif 22: Permitted through a non-Jew. And public building is forbidden even through a non-Jew [R' Akiva Eiger in the name of Shakh Knesset HaGedolah] — it is not clear.",
  "559|chokhmat-shlomo|6|_": "Seif 6. If there is a mourner in the city who goes to the synagogue at night, etc. The Panad will decide between the 3rd and the night because Dain Merbin in mourning will not go to the synagogue, but on the day since Demerbin is in mourning, this must be attached to a model in the ARA. Decision and check:",
  "559|rabbi-akiva-eiger|9|_": "Seif 9: And is not supplementary. And also for one who is slightly ill and a pregnant woman who has slight discomfort — they are permitted to eat on Yom Tov.",
  "560|rabbi-akiva-eiger|2|_": "Seif 2 in the gloss: and there are places that practiced to break. And likewise Tosafot Berachot 31a.",
  "561|chokhmat-shlomo|2|_": "Seif 2. The temple means the house of our sanctuaries and our glories, etc. P.S. Refer to the Magen Avraham Skab that the Rambam and Rabbad group brought on the issue of whether the holiness of Jerusalem and the MK is sanctified to the Almighty or not. And refer to my essays on Yod Mahdura 16 Siman 23 in the Laws of Bechor in Yod C. 2006 where I brought a question about the Rambam from Sect Damgala page 10 of the 10th and daf",
  "561|rabbi-akiva-eiger|3|_": "Seif 3: And if he tore on Jerusalem apparently this is obvious — also cities of Judah that he already tore on one of the cities of Judah, he does not return and tear when he sees the rest of the cities of Judah. And he proved this from Sefer Ta'avat La'ayinayim. And if he tore on Jerusalem first, he need not tear at all on the rest of the cities of Judah, and even in additions he does not tear. And per the Mechaber's wording if he tore on Jerusalem first, he need not tear at all on the rest of the cities of Judah.",
  "561|rabbi-akiva-eiger|4|_": "Seif 4: Until he finds his heart. Per Rambam and Tur — this is the words of Ramban on Shabbat 20b that every tearing is a tefach — the Shulchan Aruch contradicts.",
  "565|chokhmat-shlomo|5|_": "Seif 5: An individual may not say the thirteen attributes. See in Rosh Pesachim ch. 3 what he explained from the Yerushalmi — he holds like R' Yehuda. And the Yerushalmi's reason: monetary. And therefore here the law is that even for d'oraisa we rely on sefek sefeka all the more so for d'rabbanan.",
  "566|rabbi-akiva-eiger|6|_": "Seif 6: And if the kohen did not fast. If in synagogue the kohen and levi did not fast and they called the kohen and afterward remembered they did not fast — if they follow him, the levi should not follow him; if they call another levi who did not fast, or an Israelite who fasts, he answers. I wrote in a responsum.",
  "570|rabbi-akiva-eiger|3|_": "Seif 3: On one who swore and ate. In responsa Rashba siman 214: possible to say d'oraisa prohibition of fasting on Shabbat; Maggid Mishneh Laws of Yom Tov ch. 4: even on Yom Tov of Shabbat and Yom Tov of Yom Tov — one sanctity. If so, even on the night of the second Yom Tov he will not eat it since he needs it for the night of the second Yom Tov. Maggid Mishneh: one who swears not to eat on Shabbat is exempt from fasting on Shabbat; on Yom Tov it is forbidden to roast on Yom Tov since he needs it for the night of the second Yom Tov — see there.",
  "574|chokhmat-shlomo|4|_": "Seif 4. It is a mitzvah to starve one's self in a famine year and it is forbidden to use one's staff, etc. P.S. I was asked by KKK Harimlov why we don't customarily forbid the use of tashmish in two hunger pangs and the questioner writes that it is possible that we rely on the MSH of the fruit of magdim in the name of the AR and that the Shabbat is not a dagzerinan daf one who is not bar most willing to call for the name of a Nami mitzvah does not interpret it and in my opinion it is a transgression that a person slaps on the heels of a dachion there are some sides that are allowed such as on the night of baptism or for children in the dark and since dishing roads that are conducive to this Totally allowed. And here's what Rom asked how much is considered starvation. It seems to me that it depends on the law in which way it is permissible to leave Israel abroad, because holding an Oona is a mitzvah and a yeshiva yeshiva is a mitzvah for that per the MD. Precisely if he does not find a likha at all, then he is allowed to leave the Nami Datz to withdraw only when he does not find a likha at all. But per the MD Daff if there were D. Sain in the rock, it is allowed to leave the H.D. And did not Nima DAC only when he did not find a taker at all? Why did Levi serve as his staff? DIL Diosef thought that per the way of nature there was not enough grain for the whole world, in particular for the two seasons of famine, only the truth is that a blessing will be sent in his hands, but Joseph in his righteousness was not sure that he would send a blessing in his hands and yet there would not be any grain in the world, so he interpreted it. For this purpose Levi served as his staff and I Shafir and Dok VIL even Diosef knew that it would be enough from any place, this is the way of a miracle. Sim only from eating because it is faded and open-minded, this prohibition is not better than all the prohibitions of the Torah, all of which are rejected because of open-mindedness, but if you use it, it is an act of miracles. In it he met, etc. Refer to Magen Avraham, what is disputed on the B.I. But refer to Maggid Mishna Petu of Mahlakot 12 and Sota 127 from there it is proven that Rabbi Yochanan Baha Dasha does not conceive until close to her baptism and refer to Mi Nada's compositions on No. Nada page 10 12 and Doc:",
  "582|chokhmat-shlomo|8|_": "Seif 8 does not say times for rejoicing, holidays and times for celebration, etc. P.S. Look at the Magen Avraham SKZ. And here in the Haftorah is the wording that Hashem our Hashem gave us for holiness and rest for honor and glory, etc. and to me it is puzzling how it belongs to say for holiness and rest since it is permissible to do food for the soul as the rest of the Yot and why in the rest of the Yot 11a for holiness and rest and here the rabbi say and I would not be able to correct it in the language that the our Hashem Hashem gave us This Day of Remembrance is to be remembered for honor and glory and we were because of the power of this day it is given for remembrance thus it is called the Day of Remembrance as the Sages said so that your memories may rise before me for good and as the bard of the Hashem at dawn on Sunday and it is made a law of remembrance for Sunday and the last day as well as in the recollections of the prayer of the addendum as it is expressly stated that this day is given for remembrance it belongs to you to say that Hashem our Hashem has given this day of remembrance In memory of honor and glory and well documented:",
  "582|rabbi-akiva-eiger|5|_": "Seif 5 in the gloss: only finished the blessing when he mentioned Hashem's name — see below siman 682 seif 1.",
  "585|chokhmat-shlomo|2|_": "Seif 2: One who kindles should bless on the rightmost candle. See in my compositions on Orach Chayim year 5644 daf 17a for proof from the right. And see Magen Avraham siman 671 s.k. 17.",
  "586|rabbi-akiva-eiger|12|_": "Seif 12 in the gloss: and the same law applies. See Shulchan Aruch below siman 695 seif 9.",
  "586|rabbi-akiva-eiger|21|_": "Seif 21: But it does not ascend. See Beer HaGolah Levush siman 475 letter 1.",
  "590|chokhmat-shlomo|4|_": "Seif 4: Three shevarim and teruah need not be in one breath. The reason poskim wrote in his name: groaning and wailing cannot be done in one breath. It appears to me the reason is not from reasoning alone before R' Eliezer's doubt in the Gemara — if so, even for R' Eliezer who permits by reasoning alone, groaning and wailing are different and the Gemara only challenged R' Yehuda.",
  "591|chokhmat-shlomo|6|_": "Seif 6 There is a challenge to what it is customary to complete shofar verses with a verse and on the day of your happiness that did not mention a shofar but trumpets and the Rabbis and Rabbis wrote to uphold the custom. P.S. Here is the opinion of the Rash, which was written by the Prophet, and I say that one should not change the currency of the blessings that were practiced in the early days, and that it is not necessary to change the currency of the blessings that were practiced in the early days, and that it is not necessary to change the currency of the blessings that were practiced in the early days, and that it is not possible to change the currency of the blessings that were practiced in the early days, and that it is not necessary to change the currency of the blessings that were practiced in the early days, and that it is not necessary to change the currency of the blessings that were practiced in the early days, and that it is not necessary to change the currency of the blessings that were practiced in the early days, and that it is not necessary to change the currency of the blessings that were practiced in the early days, and that the most important thing is that without a shofar, the shofar of the N.V. is blown, and that is why the inscription of the trumpets in the Korah does not sound the Akal, and it is surprising to me from the Shas Dara, page 29 of the Decamere. And the dictation of a day of celebration shall be to you from Man Delita, but by the blowing of the day of the day, it is obligatory, and I am a priest, since I am a priest, and I am steadfast in the blowing of the trumpets throughout the year. A deed of remembrance is one from the other. As a reminder, we mention a shofar and not trumpets, and yet since the text of takiya includes even a shofar, the same also applies to the act of nima, since we are united in takiya and takiya is a shofar. And here, being in this, it is difficult for me, what is the crispness of the Shas Peshita, he said so to the above page 27, all the shofarots are kosher except for the cow, because it is horn and kamer there, Ola Datama d'rabbanan, oh, from the strength of Dain Katigor, we become a defender and a crisp there, and because it is a horn of Katani, and from Mhani Hada, and more Kamari Rabnan Hada Dain Katigor, we become an advocate, and more because it is a horn, and not a shofar, etc. What is wrong here because the intent of the Tana is that they are all obligated to blow the shofar precisely and not go out because of a cow which is a faded horn S.D. The main reason according to them is that a shofar is made by virtue of a claim category and so on priests and Levites who have not sinned in it they will go out even because of a cow for this KML the main reason is because it is. 11 In other words, per the truth, it was not permissible for him to say yes on behalf of the Tama d'rabbanan there, and like a spoon, and like gold clothes, and it was not permissible for him to be excused there. But per the SAD Shafir, it is possible to say yes, and it is SAD, priests and Levites will be permitted even in the horn for this, KML Dvamet, there is no Tama Lita, only the taste Daikri Kern and Tza and Dok. A challenge to the Rabbi and the Rabbis and the Korim who did not see the words of the book was brought to Bilkot in Parashat Ba Ha'altach in the verse and they sounded the trumpets and the late Rabbi Natan says OT and they sounded the trumpets of the Shofarots. After all, this is explained in the Deza Hoi Shuferot and explaining the intent of the book, we wrote in the Darosh for the year 2014 and the year 2016 and the year 1987 and the year 2015.",
  "591|rabbi-akiva-eiger|4|_": "Seif 4: And ten of shofar in every blessing. And one says: hears the sound of rejoicing with His people Israel today in mercy. On Shabbat do not say today. And in the Garden of Eden one says: hears a remembrance of rejoicing with His people Israel.",
  "607|rabbi-akiva-eiger|3|_": "Seif 3 in the gloss: but we have sinned. In Shelah Darla it is written: the wording in his eyes is correct, but we and our fathers have sinned.",
  "608|chokhmat-shlomo|2|_": "Seif 2 of the 15th and 15th of the Amirinan Prohibition in all things, it is better for them to be shoggin and not to be misadventures, etc. P.S. Here in the law it is better for them to be shoggin and not to be mazidin. And check: (Ibid. in the Haga) and indeed it is not explained in the Torah, even if it is Dauriyta, but if it is explained in the Torah, Muchin, in the hand of the rabbi, K.L. Toba from the Shas, Dibmoth, a page from Gabi Yeshai, the prophet Demidan Dayna, and Katli' Damrinan, there Dishai said, \"Ima Lia Ashviyya is deliberate and difficult, then murder to kill a soul for nothing is explained in the Torah, and everything that the prohibition is explained in the Torah does not Amerinan, it is better that they be Shoggin and not Mazidin, etc. Why did Yeshai say Ima Lia Ashviya Mazid. And here it would have been possible to justify Danyahu's prohibition of murder from the Torah, MM, also to the contrary, to kill a false prophet, as from the Torah, as a mitzvah, only what is it that it was forbidden to kill by the power of Dishai', he would have justified her soul as the truth, and if so, since her religion is not explained in the Torah, the prohibition is not called explicit in the Torah. But here, apart from this being urgent, what else can be done to the Mash HaTos in Yavem there, in the name of Rabbi Shishai, he wanted to return to him a model in Moshe Kativ and they will see the Hashem of Israel. And it is possible that with this intention the Toss brought the words of the rabbi there to the permissible because it is necessary to say that he wanted to reply to him as it is interpreted in the Shas here and here in the especlaria that illuminates, etc. But they mean it Ruth per the words of the Rabbi who had it in his power to reply to him in the readings of the Torah and they did not answer, and from this it is proven that they should be shoggin and not be scumbags for this, however, it seems that the rabbi in the name of the Rabbi will be divided on the opinion of the judges brought by the Magen Avraham and they are of the opinion that the Torah is explicit, it is better for them to be a shoggin now, etc.",
  "610|rabbi-akiva-eiger|4|_": "Seif 4 in the gloss: they are accustomed not to wear gold on Yom Tov. And there are places that drill and do not wear gold on behalf of not importing a prosecutor — see there.",
  "612|rabbi-akiva-eiger|6|_": "Seif 6: Foods that are not fit for eating. See siman 567 in Magen Avraham.",
  "612|rabbi-akiva-eiger|9|_": "Seif 9: Less than a revi'it. We were most of the revi'it — see above siman 471 seif 3.",
  "613|chokhmat-shlomo|2|_": "Seif 2 of the Haga, and it is not considered for the pleasure of bathing, only to pass the evil spirit over the hands. NB: What is wrong with that, if it is intentional, then it is not possible, and if it is intentional, then it is necessary to bathe because of the power of the evil spirit, and what is the difference if it is also intentional for the pleasure of it, and it is not allowed? The act and since the act must be done if it is intended also for his pleasure. And it is even more difficult than the Shas Decamer in P.H. Dumas because Peligi in no way considered a serious grave contribution and in the MS a grave contribution, etc. He will be stuck because of many people who are enjoying themselves when they are stuck, and one must feel that it is also aimed at his pleasure, and so the NIV. If we eat a dip, it is the pleasure of the view. With this allocation for his pleasure and in any case since he has a tax that he sets aside first, he will have a tax to be used for his pleasure and in any case also with a non-ethical donation to be used for his pleasure only according to p.n. And on the other hand, S.R. Yehuda, why is it not possible and why is it possible that the ministers of Didia, as a mash in the PvB, are going to pass for this, how did S.D. Or DSL dela haishinen Shaikhon also for his pleasure and from Nal d Sal Tabel Hamor. And it is even more difficult from the method of the Rabbinical Rabbis and the Rabbinate if you butcher a patient or feed him carrion. And the MT did not mention that, therefore, we butchered him. The reason for this is that it is not a sal as a gmayi in this only if it is intended to be prohibited. And it is possible to divide a Dadauka in a Dava bath to pass away an evil spirit and not for the pleasure of the body at all, and in this he must not pretend to be for the pleasure of the body. A special treatment for a patient who finally eats the creation of his body to revive his soul and since finally enjoying his body is allowed, even if he is not prepared for it, just for his pleasure.",
  "616|rabbi-akiva-eiger|2|_": "Seif 2: A healthy minor. See responsa Taz on Har HaChayyim siman 111.",
  "617|chokhmat-shlomo|2|_": "Seif 2. Embryo, smelling, etc. P.S. Refer to the MGA who wrote a statement, \"I need you, even if her face does not change, she is faithful.\" And here we look to bring evidence to his words from an explicit Mnana 5H Davot in ten miracles that was performed for our ancestors in the Bahmak Daka' where a woman who smelled holy meat had never aborted and brought there the Toitat in the name of Rashi that if she had smelled holy meat they would not have given her holy meat. And here in the New Testament they were puzzled about him with an explicit question on the Yoma Dampuresh where a deobrah that smelled holy meat was wrapped in a sauce and placed in the koshya on Rashi. And here in the page there he brought in the name of the Gaon, the Hashem Shaul, the late Deityan Dabash Dayin, she is clearly pregnant, she is not faithful to Him, that she smelled, etc. And it does not seem so, since DAA is faithful to the prohibition, what do I do if a pregnant woman is held who trusts Him that she smelled, and we do not suspect her of lying, and what do I do if she says she is pregnant, and why does she trust Hashem and not Hashem. That she will get pregnant. And it seems that Dedino is not true, she is pregnant, and Rashi's intention is that Rashi's grammar is correct, the additions are difficult for Rashi. And the good that he did not bring down. But it is difficult for Rashi Delma to consider this one of the miracles that were done to our ancestors in the BHMC. It is a miracle that he did not bring down the world's good in the BHAMK. But C.L. Datova and Hans was for the BHMC If we would not need a holy place for her and if she had miscarried, she would have been consumed in the holy place for her, then this is considered a miracle in the BHMC. But it is so difficult why he did not miscarry, Hashem did not smell, for the sake of the world, he took what was a favor to them that she did not miscarry, but if they thought the miracle has something to do with the holy, then the holy is the miracle that she did not smell and Hashem did not smell. And in this case, Rashi is required to say, \"No, it's a miracle that she didn't smell, only a miracle. It's a miracle if the smell didn't fall, and in any case, it's a good thing for the holy place. If the smell wasn't given to her, there's a fear of danger because it's held that she'll never fall for it.\" per the Holy Book, there is a penalty for this only as another person. If they have a change of face, then they give her a dove. The danger is from her body as a person, and the fear is for her body, not for the child. But if her face does not change, she only says, \"No, we do not give it to her, because her face has not changed, and for the child, there is no fear because it is strong. that the smell would not have been given to her and we would not have desecrated the holy place and it is considered a miracle of the Bahmak and Ash according to Rashi's method, after the miracle they would not have been given to her and it is proven by the power of the miracle, but if it were not for that they would have been given to her even by her speech alone and by this it is proven yes. The Toitat Rish Yoma Dha and Ho From a verse that wrote like this, another priest will rule under him from the power of Dain, relying on the miracle. Only if you do not fear a prohibition even in 23 there is no somkin, but if you do a prohibition then somkin on the miracle and 22 and according to Panad and daf And here in Shas Diuma is the source of this law, in which it goes through that the interpretation of Pirshi Dihoch Hoi and ends in S. Shemot in the land of the similitude of KML Rashi in this, it is interpreted as such in Shas and it is strongly emphasized there. And for Panad ASH very deliberate Rashi with the exception of the additions of Yohak because the additions of Yohak Dauriita Hois SD model in this if she smelled a whisper in the ear Duff Dampursh in Shas and Lakman C. Tarikh women who eat until dark there is no brain in their hand MM Hois SD that is, precisely if she eats herself food if she smelled for the reason that she is not allowed to eat and in particular Deza Hoi because of Hold will be obliged to whisper in her ear. Therefore, the interpretation of this is only a deza meiri in Yohak itself, but in the additions of Yohak, since they are brainstorming in their hands if they take it from themselves, not in the whispering of Yohak. And it is possible that this means to Rashi Dam, in the additions of the Hashem Hashem, it is not true that wicked men shed mercy on her, because it is an explicit rule in the Torah, and if the women eat, there is no brain in them, lest he be called evil for this, and in the Hashem Hashem himself, I take a close look at it:",
  "619|chokhmat-shlomo|2|_": "Seif 2: On Yom Kippur and the day after they say Shema Yisrael aloud. See in my compositions on Orach Chayim year 5644 I brought explicit proof from Midrash Tanhuma on Vayechi.",
  "622|chokhmat-shlomo|2|_": "Seif 2: And they read three sections — Re'eh, you shall say, and on the day of your joy. See Taz on why we complete Shofarot verses with the verse and on the day of your joy. The third haftarah is Jonah — poskim agree the custom is to uphold it.",
  "624|chokhmat-shlomo|5|_": "Seif 5 in the gloss: some are stringent to observe two days of Yom Kippur. See in my compositions on Orach Chayim year 5644 daf 102b what I wrote regarding R' Shaul Rapaport and Rashi on Rosh Hashanah 21a.",
  "629|chokhmat-shlomo|6|_": "Seif 6: In a mat of reeds and straw. See Yeshuot Yaakov who doubts whether to thatch with this in our times in Spain — see there.",
  "629|rabbi-akiva-eiger|12|_": "Seif 12: Their law is like invalid sechach and they disqualify. In my humble opinion only to be stringent — for if they are invalid sechach, since they receive tumah and join to invalidate, and since they are thin like invalid sechach, they are muktzeh and do not join to invalidate in d'rabbanan as in siman 632 seif 3. And proof: when they are placed in a sukkah, even if not for sechach, they are forbidden to benefit from — see Magen Avraham. And when invalid, all the more so. And when placed for sechach, even if not for sechach, they are forbidden to benefit from — see Taz. And when not for sechach, if they are not placed for sechach, they are permitted to use for need of the sukkah.",
  "629|rabbi-akiva-eiger|6|_": "Seif 6 in the gloss: that is, the majority of the city. Not like Tur who wrote: the majority of the city makes a sukkah for sechach. And when made for sechach, even if the majority does not make for sechach, since he intends for sechach, it is permitted.",
  "629|shaarei-teshuvah|16|_": "Seif 16 in Shulchan Aruch: It is not proper to make a sukkah from bundles of wood called fashini in Spain. They tie them so they dry quickly and place them on the fire and reduce effort gathering them one by one. And there is not twenty-five reeds in each bundle per Magen Avraham. One may permit in rabbinic law since there is no melacha concern — see there.",
  "630|rabbi-akiva-eiger|3|_": "Seif 3: He had two walls. See responsa Rama ch. 2 siman 4.",
  "630|shaarei-teshuvah|10|_": "Seif 10 in Shulchan Aruch: It is not proper to make a sukkah from sheets. If one cannot make with reeds, one may be lenient per Taz and others; see there.",
  "631|rabbi-akiva-eiger|5|_": "Seif 5: The language of the Rambam: in the air between the upper and lower reed. And in the name of Ri'etz Giat: also in the air between the upper and lower reed. If there is reed width above, it is sufficient in the air between the lower reeds even if not in the upper — see there. The Rambam implies if placement accomplishes the mitzvah, extinguishing is not required — see Magen Avraham.",
  "631|rabbi-akiva-eiger|9|_": "Seif 9: That the boards are not four amot wide. Ri'etz Giat in Machloket Menachot: boards not four amot wide are not considered independent; Ramban: halacha follows R' Meir that schach must be independent. If so, when cutting wheat found white flour, certainly forbidden even before Pesach — see there.",
  "633|yad-ephraim|9|_": "Seif 9: If its heat is greater than its cold, it is invalid even b'dieved unlike some poskim — see Ritva.",
  "634|rabbi-akiva-eiger|3|_": "Seif 3: And we reduce it. See Beit Yosef siman 506 seif 3: the reason is since it is forbidden to take all seven days; if not separated, it is forbidden to take on Yom Tov. If separated, permitted. And in Mishbetzot Zahav: reason for prohibition on Yom Tov is muktzeh from Shabbat — see there.",
  "638|chokhmat-shlomo|2|_": "Seif 2, as well as those who eat and drink in the Sukkah in order to be proper, etc. P.S. Here the author just wrote a sukkah denoi that is forbidden and does not sail whether it is within D to thatch or not. And in my update on the issue of a sheet being placed on it and in my update to C. 1879 I wrote there evidence from the Shas Deduka that faded within D to thatch in the matter of Dakshra the Sukkah even though the thatch is a wide thing then the ornament is prohibited but if it exceeds D. The ornament is a broad thing, in any case, it is not invalid regarding the sukkah and it is not considered as a thatch, nor is it forbidden in pleasure, and even in the sides of the Sukkah, it is forbidden, since they are adjacent to the wall and are an ornament to the side, but if it is not an ornament to the side, they only hang on the thatch and are far from the thatch, then they are permitted in pleasure because I extended this and the ruling on the judges who covered it: Seif 2 of the Haga and to me it seems that Damshom damsots the sukkah of each person and his house and his wife, etc. P.S. Refer to the 16th chapter of the discussion of the Rama's Setri Ahdadi from the siman 20. And Panad does not seem to have any contradictions at all, and when Abar in Aza'i and from the beginning we point out that this is required from the Shas Duff Sha'i to sleep in the Sukkah MM is obligated in the Sukkah to eat and drink and he is from what they have left in the Sukkah a page of M3 and a Sukkah body from NL, etc. until the end of the issue 11, you shall return to the GZS. After all, in the sukkah, he must also sleep there, and he does not belong in the reserve, otherwise he is allowed to sleep there, and the Panad seems to have an excuse for its company. And he is certain that he has to sleep there, and he must be obliged day and night, only the DHS is sufficient for her. Yes, Dain can sleep there at night if he has to sleep during the day and at night, or is he not obligated only on that day, there is no sleeping time, of course he is obligated, but at night, the sleeping time is faded, if he cannot sleep there, he is allowed to defor even a mask, and this is peppered there. Even at night, when he sleeps, he will sit down as if he were to sleep. Who is Sal Leh Mordechai, because in some places his image is faded in his hand, even a way of prohibition is called by him, because the the matter is in his hand to sleep there, only because he could not sleep because of the commandments of the Torah. There, only Dainu wants to keep the mitzvah of the season, and because of the mitzvah of the season, he refrains from sleeping there, while in the case he slept there, he is obliged to eat in the sukkah even at night, but because he is sorry, etc., he cannot sleep there. By eating it didn't come out and therefore I don't contradict the words of the Rama Ehadadi and rather evidence thus the Rama wrote this taste and in the first taste he wrote because of the cold it was hard for him the difficulty of the 16th but even in eating it didn't come out giving this taste he can sleep there and similar to the Meloym and the Ash and the Dok as in the Nalapand is it correct to reconcile the things? What made it difficult was the words of the Shas Ehadadi and I saw in my citations for the 2015 edition of the issue there, in the settlement, this difficulty is about correctness and the citation there Tlia if AA to sleep if it came out by eating or not, and D.O.K.:",
  "639|rabbi-akiva-eiger|7|_": "Seif 7: Rain on Yom Tov. In Tzelach Berachot 39: wait until rain stops then eat more than a kezayit in the sukkah and bless. If he sees rain while eating, he returns and eats more than a kezayit and bless — see there.",
  "640|rabbi-akiva-eiger|4|_": "Seif 4: If there is great trouble. And he is distressed at all. And in Levush: even if he is distressed at all, it is permitted to go to his friend's sukkah.",
  "643|chokhmat-shlomo|3|_": "Seif 3 in the gloss: invalid even in pressing need and even if there is an opinion that permits — see Taz, Magen Avraham, Chayei Adam, Kaf HaChayyim. In our countries they practice thus.",
  "646|rabbi-akiva-eiger|8|_": "Seif 8: If of the three, one leaf on top of two — appears he requires three kinin on the hadas. And if one leaf is on top of two, it is invalid — see Rosh. And I found that the gaon Maharal of Vilna zt\"l agreed. It still puzzles me — requires study. The Shulchan Aruch ruled like Rosh on seif 3.",
  "647|chokhmat-shlomo|2|_": "Seif 2. A willow that has been disturbed, or that most of it has fallen off, or that its head has been cut off improperly, etc. P.S. Here Garsinan in Shas Dasukah took his head from the Yot and a Tamara rose in it at the Yot, etc. There is a delay in matzot, etc. And here is the spring in the Shas, it will be seen to heal a dam, if a tamarind rose in it from Ayot, even a delay from Iqra, only if a tamarind did not rise in it until Yot, no, it would be a delay from Iqra. And the spring there, Gabi Dashhor will be sick, a proven fact, there, dam, it will blacken before its time. And here is why one must wonder about the Rambam's laws regarding the prohibitions of the altar, P. 5, who wrote that the water of the holiday was defiled and purified and purified, and as will be explained in Purification, if Tiharan and later were defiled because they were rejected, they will be rejected. And here is what is puzzling about it from Ha Devashas in the Pb Dapaschi', it is explained that it is a power from above and the Rambam took it as a power of delay, it is puzzling to me and I found it later in the book of Salach that it is difficult. But it is even more puzzling to me, because KIL whatever he has in his hand is not a delay and since David has to shikan why would he consider a delay. But another K.L. because the Rambam said, \"Who is the holiday that is defiled, meaning Dabar, the holiday falls like this, even without the Kadishan, it will be seen and rejected by the Tem in Lulav, Lika Kadesh, and even the blood is proven by Marashi, Shem Daduka, Bater Dagadiya, it is seen and rejected, or a delay from the Iqra, but without an Agadia, it is not considered a delay of MM. And from M.M. because it was an invitation, it was a delay, because the Rambam said, \"Who is the holiday?\" If his body is faded for the mitzvah, then the invitation is not a malta, it is a MM hoi delay. But on the days of the holiday, even if he goes to the name of a holiday, there is no noticeable change from his body, if it is for a holiday, then it is not considered a delay. And with this, it would be possible to settle the Rambam's words from the above-mentioned Kushit Salach, and Nami Koshiyati will sit down, faded in his hand to correct, and he is a dna', of course, to Lep May Dki'l, an invitation to Malta is for that, even a Damalan for the sake of a holiday. It is not considered rejected only because the change is evident in his body, but for the Madd, an invitation from Lata is certainly also evident from his body, because it is faded from Lata, it is a delay, as in Lulav Agud, and therefore Rabbi Yeremiah in the Shas Shem DSL, the taste of this is because of the power of the SAL. Damlan for the sake of the Dakran holiday, and for the sake of DSL, this is not a delay due to the power of David to correct it. And in this case, the reason is because of the power of Hashem, they did the sanctifications. To this he is the one who in the beginning in this is considered to correct in his hand because it is faded in his hand to correct, but in this case the water that was defiled because it was washed does not have purity in the mikvah and the permission to sow is due to the cancellation of faded such that they were canceled and made connected to the ground, so those who were purified are considered as PFH and if so the LSH is in his hand to correct what was You start when they are defiled, it is not in his power to fix it, only for him to come to Laban, it is not considered to be in his power to fix, so the first ones were rejected, and since finally these are from the first ones, it is considered a delay, and first, the kadishen, since it was an invitation to Malta, is not considered a delay at all, and as I was at it, I saw another statement of the Rambam, later in Hig Hatin, which is smaller than cattle dung and semen, so it is doubtful if she passed Meowsen, etc. And here they are true in this, Hoi Abia in Shas Menachot, etc. But K.L. Why didn't he divide between the seed and then the kadishan, or the kadishan, it will be valid, and M.M.N. In this case, the issue of the Shas in the Kadishan will begin, and then the seed will begin. If Meos is a seed of impurity or better than impurity and he himself is satisfied with what the problems of the Shas are, therefore just in every matter he is doubtful and he is also in this:",
  "651|rabbi-akiva-eiger|5|_": "Seif 5: He blesses on taking the lulav and Shehecheyanu. What he does not bless on taking date palm fronds — the language of the verse is lulav.",
  "653|rabbi-akiva-eiger|2|_": "Seif 2: And not by way. See below siman 695 seif 2 and Magen Avraham there.",
  "658|rabbi-akiva-eiger|6|_": "Seif 6: Picked before Yom Tov. See responsa Mahashat Moshe siman 4.",
  "663|chokhmat-shlomo|3|_": "Seif 3, Mu'ayin B. books in one Korin see, you say to me and Meftir calls sacrifices of Spika Duma, etc. P.S. Allegedly KL from Tractate RA [page 4.] What corruption did the Levites corrupt in the song Haka, they translated that they did not say Shira, the whole point of the Raza said that they said Shira of Hul. And apparently KL, why didn't they say there that the singing of Hul and Yot is sufficient, and why did they say only Hul alone? And the NKJV is more precise according to what you translated that they didn't say \"Shira\" all the main thing is redundant, meaning that they felt it to provide a change that provides a deficiency and even certainly a deficiency, because if they don't say \"Shira all the main thing\" is missing, the poetry would certainly be eaten if they said \"Shira of Chul\" or \"YOT\" there is only a doubt of change and yet they felt it was necessary to provide a change from a certain deficiency. If on Monday or Tuesday, etc., in the reading of our sacrifice, which does not belong today, it is a certain change and they will not feel it. And he doesn't have one, since they both state that it is not a change from Sqqa, but a change is not considered, even the Tem will say that they are both Saqqah of Hul and of Yot. And apparently it is possible to divide between the fact that the matter stands and it has become clear or not the fact that Dainu is going to clarify afterwards immediately in the near future if they say to Tervayahu it will become clear that they said one thing, certainly not in a fair way, and it is a disgrace to the Levites and their sacrifice. Who is in this? It was not left to me to settle, why did you translate this, that they did not say at all, but to the Raz, they said that it was a hoax, etc. It will finally turn out that they said that it is not fair, it is difficult, both of them. They are true, this is not true. It's hard to prove that it wasn't fair, they weren't afraid, even if it turns out later, but if they both say it, and it turns out that one of them said that they didn't believe in it, it's a good feeling to clarify the matter, but it's really hard for the Raz to say that of Hul, and not what the YoT and the Hul said, since today, Demspeka, Len Baspika Diuma, prays for the Yot and does the Yot that is sufficient, and they would also practice the sacred from the beginning of the day, and why for everything They held him to the Jut Sufficient and the singing of Chul's Ho'l to the Rabbi's Sufficient, and even if it turns out later that they said it was not fair, even if they said that Chul's Hoi was yes. And he doesn't have a dishal of sand, Hoy Tadir Daza Lash, just for the sake of going forward, but to postpone the second one entirely, I wouldn't say that Tadir is better [and refer to my new essays 3.15 of the 25th month of this and refer to the Tosafs 52 Dashab and 16 C. 151 1918 and the son] It is possible that they should be known for fear that the witnesses will not come until tomorrow, and that tomorrow is holy, and if they hear that they said a song of Yot, they will believe that it is certainly today Yot and they will make the day after tomorrow for this they made known to him a song of sand in this and still Tsa: And here per the above it is proven from his translation that they are more afraid to provide a change than to provide a lack. It seems to settle what Mordechai struggled with in the scroll, why are we not Korin, the revelation in the siman 10, from the fact that we are not in Kibiya Dircha. And per the above A.S. Dam, we interpret the Megillah in the 2nd, there is no doubt that there is a change Why is it not Purim and there is no doubt about change, but if today is Purim and we do not recite the Megillah in it, there is a doubt that there is a shortcoming, and it is more necessary to feel to supply a change than to supply a shortcoming, therefore there is no obligation to read the Megillah. And it is not similar to what we do YoT B. Days are sufficient and we pray for YoT. It's because you're a preacher, even Shoggin, you're false, and so on, when we do two days, it's really considered a YoT, and there's no doubt that it's a change, only if it's really in the YoT. And what's more, the main part of the YoT is in the prohibition of doing work and prayer. It is not a change at all, and since it is forbidden in the work and in this particular, it is doubtful of the Torah and forbidden in the work ACP, and since it is a faded day that is forbidden in the work, it has the Torah of the New Testament and belongs to the prayer of the New Testament and is holy per the New Testament law. But in the scroll the main thing is only the reading of the revelation and the feast and the rejoicing and the sending of portions and this is all about getting up and doing and it is not at all the times and the Lash in which to Him you are Efi Shoggin and for this they felt Hashem more to provide a change than to provide for a deficiency and for this there is no Korin of the Megila 15 which provides a Vash and Dok and look at the evidence for the above-mentioned sabra Divot 9. RA why is it not said Shabbat Shabbat where the scripture opens Tachla and Rashi interpreted and he came to warn about the work that is the main thing and thus it is good to demand, etc. Akal. So it is proven that the Daff for the prayer of malkiots and remembrances is from the Torah. This is a major craft. And that's why the desalination continued after her anyway. But in the scroll Hoi to reverse is the main thing for this, aa to correct, to happen is sufficient, as in the above, it is true and accurate:",
  "671|rabbi-akiva-eiger|8|_": "Seif 8: Sufficient for him with one of them. Per Rosh — requires three kinin on the hadas. And Maharal of Vilna agreed.",
  "675|rabbi-akiva-eiger|3|_": "{Rama: Seif 3} And there are those who say regarding a minor who reached chinuch. See below siman 689 seif 2 and Magen Avraham there s.k. 4.",
  "676|chokhmat-shlomo|5|_": "Seif 5: Will begin to light on Sunday night from the rightmost candle. See in my compositions on Orach Chayim year 5644 daf 17a for proof from the right.",
  "685|chokhmat-shlomo|7|_": "Seif 7: Some say Parashat Zachor and Parashat Parah Adumah are d'oraisa. Magen Avraham wrote one fulfills through the positive mitzvah of remembering Amalek. If so, why in Megillah does it say we read Parashat Vayavo Amalek on Purim? It appears to me the reason is: since we hold like poskim that on Purim we read Parashat Vayavo Amalek, therefore it is d'oraisa. And what Magen Avraham wrote — that one fulfills through the positive mitzvah of remembering Amalek — is when there is no other way to fulfill. And what he wrote on Parashat Parah Adumah — that one fulfills through the positive mitzvah of Parah Adumah. And what I heard from the famous gaon Av Beit Din R' Shaul zt\"l — end of his words.",
  "688|rabbi-akiva-eiger|6|_": "Seif 6: And on the second day they read Vayavo Amalek. And if they forgot and read it, they must take out a Torah scroll and read with a blessing.",
  "689|chokhmat-shlomo|5|_": "Seif 5: If all know, each reads for himself. See Magen Avraham s.k. 10 who brought views whether another can discharge a friend in Megillah with fewer than ten — see there. At first glance difficult: why does the gemara in Megillah challenge from Megillah in its time individually with ten? It appears Rava's view: if all know, each reads for himself; if not, need ten. And per what Magen Avraham wrote, even if all know, discharge is only rabbinic — see there.",
  "690|rabbi-akiva-eiger|10|_": "Seif 10: Does not fulfill in a foreign language. And cannot discharge a gentile in a foreign language. Since one who reads is not obligated in the matter, as Jerusalem teaches, he is not obligated by the matter and the gentile is not.",
  "690|rabbi-akiva-eiger|9|_": "Seif 9 in the gloss: but there is no concern regarding which script. In responsa Chavat Yair siman 106: much to be puzzled about this; see there.",
  "693|chokhmat-shlomo|2|_": "Seif 2 in the gloss: one does not say on the miracles except on day 14 but not on day 15. See Taz s.k. 3. Rashi Megillah 7b: lo mazkerinan — we do not mention. And even though poskim agree the custom is to uphold it, nevertheless it is difficult why the Mechaber ruled like Rashi.",
  "694|chokhmat-shlomo|2|_": "Seif 2: Rambam's words on exchanging Purim money for charity are puzzling — see in my compositions on Yoreh De'ah year 5644 siman 482.",
  "696|chokhmat-shlomo|4|_": "Seif 4 in Hagahah: nevertheless he goes to synagogue to hear Megillah etc. NB I was in doubt regarding a woman — what is her law, whether she is permitted to go to synagogue to hear Megillah. What brought me to this doubt is per what a certain scholar aroused me to explain for him Rashba's responsum general 27 law 2, who wrote: and a mourner does not leave his house's door the first Shabbat to hear blessing of milah and blessing of bridegrooms; and this that is difficult for you, that a mourner is obligated in all mitzvot stated in Torah — this refers to positive mitzvot in his body such as tzitzit and tefillin or to circumcise his son; but to go to milah and canopy and to hear the blessing for the sake of gemilut chasadim he has no permission to leave his house's door — end of his words. [Brought in Shach Yoreh Deah siman 393 see there.] On this the aforementioned scholar asked: from what is written regarding Megillah, explicit here that he is permitted to go hear Megillah — per the questioner's words now. Behold at first glance it appeared to me to settle that Rashba's intent is specifically where the mitzvah is in action, such as putting on tefillin and the like — then he is permitted to go even though he nullifies mourning, since mourning is all its prohibition in shev v'lo taaseh d'rabbanan, and behold for a Torah prohibition too a positive mitzvah overrides a negative — all the more for d'rabbanan of action which is stronger; unlike hearing blessings and the like which are not action in his body — therefore anything without action in a Torah prohibition is not overridden, and the same for d'rabbanan, for whatever the rabbis enacted they enacted like d'oraisa. Therefore regarding Megillah, since here each is obligated to read Megillah and what one discharges another is by agency, as Peri Megadim wrote in Orach Chayyim and in Sefer Kaf HaChayyim, and I extended on this elsewhere — if so here, since the essence of the mitzvah is obligation on his body in action, only the agent acts and it is as him — if so here there is action, similar to tzitzit and tefillin etc. Per this it appears this is specifically for a male, for the obligation is upon him to read Megillah and what one discharges another is by agency — therefore there is action and mourning is deferred. But for a woman, since she is not obligated in reading only in hearing — if so whoever hears fulfills and she needs no agency at all — here it is like hearing blessings and mourning is not deferred; therefore she is forbidden to go out to hear Megillah since there is no action in her body. So it appeared to him. However in truth there is no proof from this to say one may not learn from Megillah reading because of other things — for behold even Torah study is canceled to hear Megillah reading, and many Torah mitzvot are deferred before Megillah reading; therefore mourning too is deferred before Megillah reading. On the contrary I wonder at those who forbid below in Shulchan Aruch for a mourner to leave his house to hear Megillah; and even those who permit here — nevertheless Rama wrote if possible to gather a minyan at home it is preferable. And why should it be so — behold Torah study, which is stringent, is canceled to hear Megillah reading, and it is clear in poskim that even if possible to read afterward in ten, nevertheless they cancel in order to hear in public. If so, if Torah study which is stringent is canceled, all the more mourning which is lighter; behold this kal vachomer itself they learned in gemara for Torah from avodah: if avodah which is stringent is canceled, Torah which is lighter — not all the more; if so this kal vachomer one may learn all the more regarding mourning which is lighter than Torah and avodah — requires study. Behold in the body of the aforementioned questioner's difficulty on the aforementioned Rashba — from what is written regarding Megillah, in any case it is not difficult, since in Shulchan Aruch there are several views whether a mourner may leave his house — one may say Rashba truly holds he does not leave his house even to hear Megillah. However one who is exacting in Rashba's responsum language will see his intent is thus: the distinction is between whether the essence of the mitzvah depends on his body, such as tzitzit, tefillin, milah where the essence depends on his body — for these we say we defer mourning before them; but a matter not done by him alone only with others' combination, such as hearing milah blessing or canopy blessing — if others were not doing the mitzvah he would have no obligation at all, and the mitzvah is not the essence of his body's obligation, only if others do the mitzvah he too is obligated to go with them to repay kindness — for this we do not defer mourning. If so automatically no question from Megillah, for there whether for men or women the obligation depends on each person's body by himself, either to read Megillah or to hear — therefore we defer mourning before them. Behold what I challenged above — why the mourner does not go hear Megillah from avodah and Torah — here one could say thus per what is written siman 232 and siman 231: one distinguishes between he began in permission and does not stop, and he began in prohibition and stops for all; if so here too what kohanim in their avodah and leviim on the platform cancel their avodah, and likewise the yeshiva of the house of Rabbi — this is when he began on Purim itself at the time the reading obligation already applied to him, therefore they are obligated to cancel; but if he began in permission he does not stop; if so mourning that applied to him before Purim — since this mourning obligation applied before Megillah — he does not stop from mourning to read Megillah. Per this it would appear if mourning did not apply to him except on Purim itself, such as buried on Purim night and the like — then for all he is permitted to go hear Megillah even if he can gather a minyan at home, nevertheless he goes to hear in public, even for those who hold mourning applies on Purim too. So it appeared to me now — requires study. Behold as I speak on this I will mention what arose in my thought to challenge on what we say in gemara there in Megillah: R' Yehoshua ben Levi said a person must read Megillah at night and repeat it by day, as written \"so that Your glory may sing and not be silent\" etc.; he thought the baraita of daytime helps; a certain rabbi told him etc. like one who passes this portion and teaches it; it is difficult — from R' Yehoshua ben Levi's language it implies his emphasis is on daytime that he is obligated to read also by day; if the emphasis were on night and by day it would be obvious to him — he should have said a person must read Megillah also at night; and since he said a person must read Megillah at night and repeat by day, it implies his main emphasis is on daytime. Difficult on daytime — why did he need the verse \"so that Your glory may sing\" etc.; and in particular how could he think from it the baraita of daytime, but hold reading obligation is not by day, and how did it enter his mind to say so? Behold since it is written mishpacha u'mishpacha we learn kohanim in their avodah and leviim on the platform and Israel in their maamad cancel their avodah to hear Megillah reading — at night there is no platform and no maamad, per force it deals with daytime reading — if so proven reading is by day. Also from kohanim in their avodah one could challenge: even though burning limbs is valid all night, nevertheless since we wrote it is proven from poskim' words that where another mitzvah applied to him before Megillah it is not deferred before Megillah — if so per force what kohanim cancel in their avodah is by day when this obligation applied to them together with Megillah, unlike burning limbs at night that applied to them from day 13 before Megillah obligation. If so one may learn kal vachomer: if mourning is not deferred before Megillah, all the more avodah which is stringent; per force it does not deal with nighttime when there is no avodah, only what remains of the day; if so per force it deals from daytime — and how could it enter his mind there is no Megillah reading by day — requires study now — requires study well. (there in that seif in Hagahah) And some say mourning does not apply on Purim, neither on the 14th nor on the 15th etc. NB from this I learned another new law regarding shmuah kerovah on Purim that in the end becomes distant — its law as explicit in Shulchan Aruch and in Shulchan Aruch Yoreh Deah siman 402: shmuah kerovah on Shabbat that in the end becomes distant — he practices only one hour; explicit there in Beit Yosef in name of Rashba's responsum the reason: even though mourning applies on Shabbat and counts even at its beginning — nevertheless here it is different, for mourning does not apply afterward except from Shabbat force while it is still the thirty-day day; if so the secondary should not be more stringent than it, for in it mourning does not apply yet its cause causes mourning — see there; if so here too since on Purim mourning does not apply, the cause should not be more stringent. Per this it appears further: even if Purim occurred after Shabbat and it is the thirty-day day on Purim and he was informed on Shabbat too — he does not have the law of shmuah kerovah, for his law is like hearing inside the festival and after the festival becomes distant. Superficially one could distinguish and say on a festival properly he has the law of distant shmuah, since on a festival mourning did not apply to him, for festival does not count in the seven — if so necessarily now mourning did not apply to him, and after the festival at the hour mourning began on him it was already distant — therefore he has distant shmuah law even though still within the thirty-day day itself. On Shabbat, even though Shabbat counts and mourning applies to him — nevertheless since on Shabbat it deals with the thirty-day day itself, and such since it is the last day and we hold part of the day is like the whole — if so if he already knew the matter mourning was already ceased from him, only that since he still did not know his law is like the day before — therefore enough to be stringent if mourning applied immediately; but if now it is Shabbat and mourning did not apply for all its laws, they were not stringent to be like the day before, and we say in it part of the day is like the whole. But if he was informed on Shabbat that within the thirty days only immediately adjacent to Shabbat is Purim, a festival on which mourning does not apply in eating, and afterward it became distant — here properly he has shmuah kerovah law, since he was informed within thirty days itself and Shabbat counts in the seven — nevertheless per Rashba's responsum reason that the secondary should not be more stringent than itself, it appears there is no distinction in the matter, and in every case mourning ceases from him since afterward it will not apply except from Shabbat force and the secondary will not be more stringent. And truthfully, without Rashba's words it would appear the reason of the matter of shmuah kerovah on Shabbat and after festival becomes distant — he practices only one hour — we were because the reason of shmuah kerovah is the day of hearing is as if before him. Therefore it appears this is specifically on a day that if he died now aninut of the first day would apply, whether before burial or after burial; therefore here it is relevant to say the day of hearing is as if before him. But on Shabbat and festival, if he died for him now aninut law after burial would not apply, as Rashba wrote in Laws of Chol HaMoed: if the first day is on Chol HaMoed he puts on tefillin — even disputants there, it is for another reason, for otherwise Chol HaMoed is not the main time of tefillin; but otherwise they would agree — if so the stringency of day one after burial did not apply to him, therefore when the essence is nullified the secondary is nullified too. But on Purim, since aninut applies to him by law — and even for those who permit meat and wine, this is only from communal positive mitzvah force, but from tefillin certainly exempt on day one even on Purim — since aninut of day one after burial applied to him, the secondary applied too; unlike Shabbat and festival, if tefillin mitzvah were relevant they would put on tefillin and would not be exempt from tefillin law from aninut after burial — if so since stringency of one day applied to him, the secondary applied too. In any case it appears since halacha follows the lenient in mourning, one may rely on Rashba's reason, especially since my ruling does not decide against Rashba's words even as a drop from the sea. Per Rashba's reason, on Purim too it is so like Shabbat; in particular since in Rama's language here Purim is mentioned like Shabbat, and also Beit HaGadol's view that Purim's law is like festivals that interrupt and do not count at all — in this and this certainly woe if his law were like shmuah kerovah on festival that after festival becomes distant that he practices only one day; therefore in this law there is doubt perhaps like Beit HaGadol and Purim's law is like festivals. And if you say not like Beit HaGadol — perhaps like Rashba's reason; if so even if his law is like Shabbat, here it is like distant shmuah law. Therefore one may rely on the halacha as written, especially in mourning d'rabbanan. So it appeared to me clear for the law — requires study well. May Hashem repair breaches with us Israel and swallow death forever and make us rejoice in His salvation; we end with holy Rama's words end of siman 697: good heart, joy always. I present here what I said in my chiddushim on Orach Chayyim year 5549 on what is brought above siman 695: a person must become intoxicated on Purim until he does not know between arur Haman and baruch Mordechai. It appears as a new matter: behold Chazal said per one view that therefore we do not say Hallel on Purim — we do not say song on a miracle outside the land. It appears this is explicit in the words of the psalmist David who said \"Who will give from Zion salvation of Israel — Jacob will exult, Israel will rejoice\" — behold the essence of song is said on joy, and if there is no joy there is no song; therefore he said \"Who will give from Zion salvation of Israel.\" If salvation were from Zion, meaning Eretz Yisrael, then Jacob would exult, Israel would rejoice; but if salvation is outside the land and not from Zion, Israel has no joy in it and there is no song. Therefore it appears also for the other view that gemara concludes in Arachin that its reading is its Hallel — since they were exiled they returned to the first permission; nevertheless they made a distinction that a miracle of chutz la'aretz and Eretz Yisrael are not equal. Therefore they enacted on Purim feasting and joy in remembrance of feasting first; and on all Yom Tov we find only mitzvah of joy and not feasting — this is like they enacted on Purim to divide honor to Eretz Yisrael in enactment of walled cities as Ran wrote; so they enacted further that all festivals made for Israel in Eretz Yisrael or before their coming to Eretz Yisrael when all lands are equal — joy alone is relevant; but on Purim when they had already merited Eretz Yisrael before, it would be fitting not to rejoice at all on a miracle in chutz la'aretz. Therefore when they enacted joy they enacted feasting first, so joy not be from the miracle alone but also from feasting, as is the way of all who drink — his heart rejoices — and joy also through feasting, to show feasting too causes joy, and we have no complete joy since the miracle was in chutz la'aretz and not of Eretz Yisrael — therefore feasting and afterward joy. From this reason too, though they enacted to read Megillah which is in place of Hallel for us who hold its reading is its Hallel — nevertheless they enacted likewise to become intoxicated on Purim until he does not know between arur Haman etc., so he forget to say the song since its reading is its Hallel. If so proven telling the miracle is the Hallel; if so automatically what is said arur Haman and baruch Mordechai is also Hallel and song; therefore obligated to become intoxicated until he does not know between arur Haman etc., so the song not be in his mouth all day and song not be complete, since it is a miracle in chutz la'aretz our joy in it is not complete — therefore song too will not be complete all day, only he forget it; unlike a miracle in Eretz Yisrael of which it is said \"When Hashem returns Zion's captivity\" — when the miracle is in Eretz Yisrael then our mouth will be filled with laughter and our tongue with rinnah all day, and we will not need to drink to forget the song, only \"my tongue shall speak Your righteousness all day, Your glory\" — and song will be complete. So may it be with Hashem's help, amen, speedily and in our days, and may the redeemer come to Zion speedily in our days, amen and amen.",
  "696|rabbi-akiva-eiger|4|_": "Seif 4 in the gloss: not on 14 and not on 15. And if 15 falls on Shabbat, mourners do not read the haftarah for comfort on Shabbat night before Ma'ariv — see Simchat HaNefesh.",
  "696|rabbi-akiva-eiger|8|_": "Seif 8 in the gloss: what they used to wear masks. See Magen Avraham siman 696 s.k. 22."
};
